Make pod startup an owned cancellable task

Startup workers could retain lifecycle locks after their clients left,
blocking recovery from stopped containers and stalled setup commands.

Own startup, reconnect, fork, and recreate as async tasks. Delete
aborts active and queued startup tasks, waits for their destruction,
then takes the lifecycle lock and removes remaining pod state. Expose
pending startup before container creation so delete can also cancel
initializers and image builds. Client disconnection detaches from
startup.

Await subprocesses and network operations throughout startup while
keeping filesystem and database work synchronous. Resource guards kill
cancelled process groups and release temporary files, connections,
agents, and proxy sessions. Keep standalone image commands in the
terminal process group so Ctrl-C also reaches builders and pull
clients.

Record unconfirmed creates durably before contacting the backend. If a
request finishes after delete observed NotFound, cleanup finds its
launch identity, including after a daemon restart. Remove containers
and networks by immutable identity and use Kubernetes UID
preconditions so cleanup cannot target a newer launch with the same
pod name. Retain uncertain intents because absence alone cannot prove
completion. Named volumes remain subject to explicit lifecycle cleanup
because they may be shared or reused.

Stream bind archives through a bounded channel from a synchronous
filesystem producer. Cancellation and early HTTP rejection release
writers blocked on the network. Drain successful uploads through EOF
so gzip finalization and archive errors cannot race with success
responses.

Bound readiness connection attempts and idle reads. Startup heartbeats
allow healthy lifecycle commands to run without an overall deadline.
Fail an established readiness stream when its container disappears.

Regression tests cover abandoned readiness waits, stopped and paused
containers, queued enters, initializer and builder descendants, host
Git fetches, failed-start cleanup, and cancellation after container
creation, late backend completion across daemon restart, terminal
signals, streaming uploads, and gzip finalization. Transport tests
preserve half-closed SSH responses and partial tunnel frames during
concurrent events.

Validation: required smoke and feature filters passed via cargo
pipeline. The full pipeline passed 603 tests with two skips and no
failures. macOS validation was unavailable because the macos SSH
hostname did not resolve.
